1. Evaluation Input / UI
This is the entry point — a simple form or interface where someone submits a question, the AI's response to that question, and optionally a reference answer or source document. Think of it as: "Here's what was asked, here's what the AI said — now check if it's good."
2. Backend / API Layer
Receives that submission, validates it (makes sure question/response aren't empty or malformed), and passes it into the system. This is your FastAPI service.
3. Evaluation Submission & Input Storage
The validated submission gets saved to a database (or in Milestone 1, a simple file) so it's not lost and can be tracked/retrieved later — each submission gets a unique ID and a status (received → processing → evaluated).
4. Reference Knowledge Base (built ahead of time, runs in parallel)
This is where "ground truth" data lives, so the system has something to check the AI's answer against:
Benchmark Dataset Ingestion — downloads TruthfulQA and SQuAD datasets (public Q&A datasets used to test/benchmark AI systems)
Data Cleaning & Chunking — breaks long reference texts into smaller pieces (chunks) so they're easier to search
Embedding Generation — converts each text chunk into a numeric vector (a way for the computer to understand meaning, not just words)
Vector DB / Semantic Search — stores these vectors so the system can quickly find chunks that are meaningfully similar to a new question, not just keyword-matching
5. RAG Retrieval Pipeline
When a new question comes in, this searches the Vector DB and pulls out the most relevant reference chunks — the "evidence" the judge agents will use to check the AI's answer. RAG = Retrieval-Augmented Generation, meaning the system retrieves real evidence instead of relying purely on the AI's own knowledge.
6. AI Evaluation Agent Layer (the core of the system)
The Agent Orchestrator sends the question, AI response, and retrieved evidence to four specialized "judge" agents, each checking one thing:
Relevance Judge — Did the AI actually answer the question asked, or go off-topic?
Accuracy Judge — Is the answer factually correct compared to the evidence?
Hallucination Detection Agent — Did the AI make up facts not supported by any evidence? (This is the core "hallucination detection" part of your project title.)
Completeness Judge — Did the AI cover everything the question required, or leave parts out?
Each of these agents is itself powered by an LLM acting as a "judge" — this is called the LLM-as-a-Judge approach.
7. Evaluation Scoring & Verdict Module
Takes the four separate scores and combines them into one final verdict — e.g., "Pass" or "Fail," or an overall score with a breakdown by dimension.
8. Structured Evaluation Results Module
Formats the final verdict + all four scores + reasoning into a clean, structured record (like JSON) so it can be stored and displayed.
9. Results & Evaluation Dashboard
The final results are shown to the user — with support for evaluating many responses at once (Batch Evaluation), generating summary reports (Report Generation), and refreshing the reference data over time (Knowledge Base Update Module).
